=== task: mcq hard
title: Subsets or orderings?
prompt: Which brute-force search tries about n! possibilities?
* Finding the best order in which to do n jobs :: Right. Every order of the n jobs is an ordering: n!.
- Finding the best group of jobs to do, from n jobs :: A group decides only in or out for each job: 2ⁿ subsets.
- Finding the two jobs that fit together best :: That's every pair: about n²/2.
- Finding the single best job :: That's one pass: n.
hint: Does the search decide **which** items, or the **order** of the items?
hint: Orderings give n!, subsets give 2ⁿ.
right: Deciding an order is the factorial kind of search. Deciding a group is exponential, deciding a pair is quadratic, and choosing one item is linear.
wrong: Only the order of all n jobs has n! possibilities. Groups are 2ⁿ, pairs about n², and single jobs n.

=== task: multi hard
title: The travelling salesman
prompt: Choose **every** true sentence about solving the travelling salesman problem by trying every route.
* With the start fixed, n places give (n − 1)! routes :: Right. The other n − 1 places can be visited in any order.
* Each route has the same length as the same route backwards :: Right. The legs are the same, in reverse.
* 20 places are far too many to try every route :: Right. 19! is about 1.2 × 10¹⁷ routes.
- Skipping the backwards routes makes it O(2ⁿ) :: It halves the work, and half of a factorial is still factorial.
- Adding one place doubles the routes :: It multiplies them by the number of places to arrange, which is more than 2 once there are 3 or more.
- Always going to the nearest place next finds the best route :: That's quick, but it can miss the best route. Only checking every route is sure to find it.
hint: Count the routes, then think about which changes really alter their growth.
hint: Three of them are true.
right: (n − 1)! routes, matching reverse pairs, and 20 places being hopeless are all true. Halving doesn't change the growth, adding a place multiplies the routes by more than 2, and the nearest-place shortcut isn't always best.
wrong: True: (n − 1)! routes, reverse routes have equal lengths, 20 places is far too many. False: halving stays factorial, a new place multiplies (not doubles), and nearest-next can miss the best route.

